  <participating-org role="4" type="24">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SANSOM MLUP PREY (SMP) was formed in 2009 to promote and market wildlife-friendly products grown or crafted in communities located in protected areas of Cambodia. It is working to link wildlife conservation to improving livelihoods of villagers limited by the constraints of living in a remote area with little opportunity to expand operations and access market.
In Cambodia, protected areas either contain, or are used by, long-established local communities who are very poor and heavily dependent upon the forest and surrounding habitat for their livelihoods.
Agricultural-based livelihood development is limited by low prices offered by the few traders that visit. This provides little incentive for communities to improve agricultural efficiency, and many households are trapped in debt cycles.
With growing human population, the pressure on land resources is increasing, leading to widespread forest clearance in key conservation areas and conflicts between communities and government agencies responsible for environmental protection.
Successful wildlife and habitat conservation depends on engaging them through tools that directly link local economic and social development to conservation.
SMP buys products from village marketing networks (VMN), whose membersare voluntarily recruited from farmers who agree to abide by conservation rules and regulations, including wildlife protection and maintenance of land-use boundaries. These rules andregulations are previously concurred to by the government and the community. They offer preferential prices to community members, and target the poorer farmers, who are often not food secure and rely on forest resources.
SMP plays a role as the villages connection to markets not typically accessible to producers from remoteprovinces and ensures that conservation activities are being adhered to, which enables rewards to be shared at the end of each season. SMP works in partnership with different organizations, including local and international NGOs, government ministries, andlocal villages.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sansom Mlup Prey</narrative>
